1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:24,160
Hey everyone, it's Tipa, and for my journal entry this month, I'm focusing in on changing

2
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:32,600
social skills and how we interact with one another when there's brain change in the system.

3
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:41,580
So with dementia, all too often people sort of go from I am who I am to my friend Harriet,

4
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:46,400
where Harriet simply represents during the pandemic.

5
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:52,820
She was my partner, my go-to if I wanted to demonstrate something like how do you shake

6
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:54,080
hands with someone?

7
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,600
How do you support someone by holding their hand?

8
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,460
How do you approach and get eye contact?

9
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:06,400
She was my role model because we were in social isolation, but in fact, Harriet's not a person

10
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,760
living with dementia.

11
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:14,680
And people living with dementia don't suddenly, in general, become unable to socialize.

12
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:20,880
And in fact, for people living with dementia and brain changes that come with dementia,

13
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:23,960
there is many social skill sets as there are people.

14
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:30,780
It turns out a lot of what might happen, might happen, or it might not happen.

15
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:35,920
It may happen early in a dementia, it may happen very late, it may barely be noticeable,

16
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:37,980
it may be right in your face.

17
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:43,400
Some of it depends on baseline, what the person was like before, how social they were, how

18
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:45,520
skillful they were.

19
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:51,420
But it also depends on so many other variables, the type of dementia, the length and duration

20
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:52,600
of having had dementia.

21
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:53,600
Yeah.

22
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:57,420
There's all kinds of abilities that you have, your ability to hear sound, to hear speech,

23
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:04,340
to interpret speech, to be able to see, see well, the ability to physically be comfortable,

24
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:09,980
the ability to use your body and to approach and to engage and feel comfortable in your

25
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:14,160
own skin and comfortable in a space and comfortable with others.

26
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:19,360
There's all kinds of comfort or discomfort someone may experience in a social situation.

27
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,260
It could be that you have some physiological distress.

28
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:24,080
There are things going on inside of you.

29
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:30,080
That's making it really difficult for you to socialize, to be social, to be social in

30
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:32,580
the way you used to be social.

31
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:35,240
And sometimes it's something physical that's happening.

32
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:39,120
And it's uncomfortable, let alone painful.

33
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:45,420
Because when we're uncomfortable or in discomfort, it makes it really hard to be totally connected

34
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:48,800
in the way I would if I were comfortable.

35
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:52,320
So I may have a facial expression, I may have a movement pattern.

36
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:53,960
I may change my icon.

37
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:58,380
I may not want to be in certain places or I may not want to be with certain people or

38
00:02:58,380 --> 00:03:00,200
I may be seeking other people.

39
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,820
Oh, yeah, my primitive brain gets in on it, too.

40
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:10,920
And the reality is there's also some elements of who we're comfortable being around.

41
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:14,160
We have different audiences that we're social with.

42
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:18,060
I mean, I have my intimate or intimates.

43
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:19,440
It could be I have one partner.

44
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:21,580
Some people have more than one.

45
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:22,580
It's an intimate partner.

46
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:23,580
And that intimate partner.

47
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:26,780
Maybe an intimate work partner, an intimate life partner.

48
00:03:26,780 --> 00:03:29,940
It could be somebody I'm physically intimate with.

49
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:36,860
So if I'm physically intimate with one person and then I make a mistake because of my ability

50
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:41,080
to take in data, I might think I'm with someone and I'm actually with someone else.

51
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:44,940
So my social behavior would be appropriate, quote unquote.

52
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:49,920
It would match what you would expect if I were with one individual in one physical setting

53
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:52,200
or social setting.

54
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:58,660
And yet it would be totally like, whoa, what is going on there in another situation?

55
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:04,300
And it could simply be my interpretation of where I am and who I'm with is different.

56
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:08,460
Or it could be that I'm actually changing and I like something different or dislike

57
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:11,260
something that I didn't used to.

58
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:16,140
Some people become more social, some become a lot less social and more selective because

59
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:21,000
we have intimates and then we have people that we know we call them friends or we call

60
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:22,000
them.

61
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:25,480
They're just familiars, but not intimates, but familiars.

62
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:29,000
They know a lot of things, but I wouldn't necessarily go around naked with them.

63
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,140
Or you know, you may not do that with anybody.

64
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:32,820
I don't know.

65
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:35,000
So I may share certain spaces.

66
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,820
I may not share other spaces.

67
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:39,600
Some people are very comfortable going into a public restroom.

68
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:44,020
And depending on your cultural background and your experiences in the world, you may

69
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:49,100
be comfortable taking care of body functions in a lot of different situations.

70
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:52,040
And some people are very uncomfortable with that.

71
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:55,260
So, you know, we may notice a change in someone.

72
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:59,480
We may notice no changes because, you know, we're not in that environment.

73
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:00,940
We're not in that situation.

74
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:04,080
But people who are in those situations might notice something.

75
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:09,520
So then we start having issues with different support personnel when somebody's brain is

76
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:14,420
changing and who's telling the truth and who's telling the truth, not telling the truth.

77
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:17,620
Maybe we're just seeing it from different perspectives.

78
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:21,800
And we really should be taking into account the perspective of the person living with

79
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:25,460
dementia a lot more often than classically people do.

80
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:28,400
So then let's take a look at acquaintances.

81
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,580
People that I know, but I just know to see.

82
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:37,520
Maybe at the store, maybe at a post office, maybe on the street, maybe in a neighborhood.

83
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:43,800
Maybe there's somebody I see in specific situations like a faith community or maybe at a sports

84
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:46,340
activity or maybe in a work situation.

85
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:48,440
I know them, but I don't know them.

86
00:05:48,440 --> 00:05:50,300
They know me, but they don't know me.

87
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:51,300
So I have certain layers.

88
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:56,000
Layers and covers and maybe even clothing and wording and certain kinds of things I

89
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,000
do.

90
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,380
And it's layers.

91
00:05:58,380 --> 00:05:59,700
It's like onion layers.

92
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:04,360
And one of the things that can happen with dementia is my layers get peeled off and I

93
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:10,840
start treating everybody like I treat one somebody or maybe I isolate a little bit more.

94
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:12,100
I compartmentalize.

95
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:16,780
And so I can be very lovely in a general public situation.

96
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:20,800
But when I get somebody alone, I'm very angry with how they treated me.

97
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:25,740
And you may see that as very different, but I feel betrayed by what was going on because

98
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:31,960
sometimes people in public settings may treat me like I'm Harriet and I'm not.

99
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:36,300
They actually talk about me without me, so I'm not even there.

100
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:41,660
They may ask my person that I'm with a lot of things or they may make comments that and

101
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:44,500
professionals do it, not just the public.

102
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:49,560
I mean, we have health care providers, legal providers, public service sector folks who

103
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:50,500
may do those kinds of things.

104
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:54,200
Because they make assumptions about things.

105
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:59,780
Now, we also have situations where due to the dementia somebody has, we might actually

106
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:07,000
see some significant changes in ability to be social, interest in being social, conversation

107
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,880
about being social and social behaviors, things that we do.

108
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:15,520
We share, we pass, we take turns, we there are so many things we do.

109
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:19,840
So for some dementias, people maintain so many social skills.

110
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:23,400
It's hard to realize how much trouble they're having doing that.

111
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:28,280
And they may be like the duck with his feet underwater, working really hard to go somewhere.

112
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:32,340
But above the water, they look like they're floating, but it's taken everything they have.

113
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:37,680
And so how long they can do something may significantly shorten.

114
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:41,740
So when in the day, how much of the time?

115
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:43,680
What's the topic of conversation?

116
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:46,620
Because there's other social considerations as well.

117
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:49,360
Some of us are more comfortable with certain intellectual experiences.

118
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:49,840
Yeah.

119
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:55,400
Like we have similar backgrounds, work experiences, leisure experiences, volunteer

120
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:59,660
experiences, family experiences, or maybe we like new and different.

121
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:05,300
And we're very different around people who are different and exciting and new and people

122
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:09,560
that are familiar, not so socially engaged with.

123
00:08:09,560 --> 00:08:14,520
It can feel like I don't like you when it's simply it's just a different social situation

124
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:15,520
for me.

125
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:19,360
Now, additionally, we may have things that are.

126
00:08:19,360 --> 00:08:26,360
Political comfort or discomfort or maybe social comfort or discomfort based on maybe

127
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:32,120
culture or race or how someone looks or how someone sounds or their accent or their appearance.

128
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:33,520
Oh, my heavens.

129
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:40,820
There's so many ways in which dementia can mess with my ability to be the social being

130
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:43,840
I used to be, or maybe I'm actually how I used to be.

131
00:08:43,840 --> 00:08:48,700
And you don't see the difference, but it's highly possible that I'm not the only one

132
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:49,240
that's changing.

133
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:53,140
Socially, when one of us is developing dementia.

134
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:57,740
So if I'm developing dementia, you may change socially and you may not notice that because

135
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:00,360
you're busy pointing out my changes.

136
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:03,920
And I would ask us to look in the mirror, look at the other person and look at the people

137
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:10,760
around and sort of let's figure out how to support one another and how to provide separate

138
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:16,520
spaces, maybe some different activities, maybe some different opportunities that may help

139
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:18,760
us all get a little more comfortable, but not.

140
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:19,760
In boxes.

141
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:24,760
I'm not really into segregation.

142
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:29,520
I think that's a mistake, but I think recognizing when someone has had enough, isn't that interested

143
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:35,740
in getting that much closer, but the idea of being present with one another and being

144
00:09:35,740 --> 00:09:41,560
supportive of one another, wherever each person is and acknowledging where we have differences

145
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:48,660
and trying to find some common ground, oh yeah, that I think those of us who have flexibility,

146
00:09:48,660 --> 00:09:53,160
and have capacity, that's where we can choose to make a difference.

147
00:09:53,160 --> 00:10:01,860
We can choose to modify and change and support and acknowledge and recognize and sigh.

